* The Model

** Emacs, VS Code, Windows, Sessions, AI-Activities

[[file:./images/vscodeClaudeHierarchy.png]]

The hierarchy, top down:

| Level          | What it is                                                                  | Cardinality                   |
|----------------+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+-------------------------------|
| Emacs (Blee)   | the running Blee server, =(expand-file-name server-name server-socket-dir)= | one, optional                 |
| VS Code        | the desktop VS Code instance of this user (=~/.config/Code=)                | one; partners with that Emacs |
| window         | a VS Code window; its folder is a *root dir*                                | many per VS Code              |
| Claude session | a conversation of the Claude extension; its cwd is the window's folder      | many per window (tabs)        |
| AI-Activity    | a directory with a =CLAUDE.md= (an /activity dir/)                          | a hierarchy per session       |

- *One Emacs, one VS Code.* For one user there is one desktop VS Code per settings directory, and
  =code= (with =--open-url=) always reaches that running instance. A second, independent VS Code would
  need its own =--user-data-dir=, and every command would have to name it; one to one is simpler, and
  it is the design.
- *Several Claude sessions per window* share the window's folder as their working directory. A
  Claude session registers in =~/.claude/sessions/<pid>.json= with its =sessionId= and =cwd=, which is
  what =vscodeClaude.cs -i claudeSessionsList= reads.

** Root and Here

A Claude session holds a hierarchy of AI-Activities (see AI-WORKFLOW.org, "The AI-Activities of a
Session: Root and Here"):

- *The root*: the session's working directory --- the VS Code window's folder --- with the top
  =CLAUDE.md=, created by =aiActivity.cs -i initiate= and confirmed by =/bx-confirm-root=.
- *Activities joined later* with =/bx-confirm-here <dir>=: an =initiateSub= sub of the root
  (homogeneous, or heterogeneous from another templates tree), or a separately initiated activity
  elsewhere.

An activity dir need not be a git repo; it is the directory that holds a =CLAUDE.md=.

=/bx-confirm-root= takes optional arguments, for Blee only. Without them it is the complete
confirmation and does nothing Emacs-related:

| Argument         | Effect                                                                         |
|------------------+--------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| =dir=<rootDir>=  | the root the sender means; a different session root is reported, and it stops  |
| =emacs=<socket>= | Claude checks it can reach that Emacs (=emacsclient … (emacs-pid)=, read-only) |
| =callback=yes=   | with =emacs==: Claude registers its session id with that Emacs                 |

** The Partnership and the Associations

/(planned: =b:vscode/init=, the partner file, the associations, =b:vscode:claude/rootRegister=)/

- *=b:vscode/init= declares the partnership.* It writes the partner file
  =/bisos/var/blee/vscode/partner-<user>.json=: the socket path, the Emacs pid, =DISPLAY=, the time.
  On exit, =kill-emacs-hook= removes it, if it is still this Emacs's. Readers treat it as stale when
  the pid is dead or the socket is gone. A second Blee running =init= takes over: one to one.
- *The partner file is for the vscode layer's own tools* --- =vscode.cs -i bleeOpenFile= (VS Code →
  Emacs) reads the socket from it. Claude does not go looking for it: a prompt names its Emacs
  explicitly (=emacs=<socket>=), so a stale partner file cannot pull a pure VS Code session into Emacs.
- *Associations: activity dir → session*, many to one, kept in Emacs:
  - =b:vscode:claude/confirmRoot= picks (or starts) the session of a root dir and sends it
    =/bx-confirm-root dir=<root> emacs=<socket> callback=yes=. Claude's callback,
    =b:vscode:claude/rootRegister=, records root dir → session id. The callback confirms that the prompt
    reached that session, and gives the id of a new conversation, which Emacs cannot know in advance.
  - =b:vscode:claude/confirmHere <dir>= sends =/bx-confirm-here <dir>= to the chosen session and
    records dir → session.
  - From a buffer, Emacs walks up from =default-directory= to the nearest associated activity dir and
    uses its session; with none, the latest session whose cwd covers the buffer.
  - One association per activity dir: the last confirmation wins. A session's id does not survive
    closing its tab: before use, a recorded session is checked against the running ones, and a dead
    one falls back to the latest in its directory, with a message.

* The Channels

[[file:./images/vscodeClaudeChannels.png]]

| # | Channel              | How                                                                                                                       | Status                               |
|---+----------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+--------------------------------------|
| 1 | Prompt in            | =vscodeClaude.cs -i claudePrompt= (=/open= URI, then paste) | works                                |
| 2 | Results back by file | Claude writes =<repo>/.git/CLAUDE_COMMIT_MSG=; magit offers it                                                            | works                                |
| 3 | Results back by call | Claude runs =emacsclient -s <socket>=, when the prompt names it                                                           | works (skill =bs-blee-emacs-server=) |
| 4 | VS Code → Emacs      | a VS Code user task and key run =vscode.cs -i bleeOpenFile=                                                               | planned (WorkPlan 10.9)              |

- *1, prompt in.* The extension's URI is
  =vscode://anthropic.claude-code/open?session=<sessionId>&prompt=<text>=. It opens the session, but a
  session that is already open ignores =prompt=, so =claudePrompt= puts the text on the clipboard
  (=xclip=) and pastes it (=xdotool=) into the focused input box. It never presses Enter: *you press
  Enter.* It needs the desktop VS Code's =DISPLAY=; without it, it refuses and names the =DISPLAY= the
  desktop runs on.
- *2, results by file.* =/bx-git-commit-msg= writes the message; =b:vscode:claude:magit:commitMsg/offer=
  (on =git-commit-setup-hook=) offers it when the commit buffer opens, and =…/markUsed= renames it to
  =CLAUDE_COMMIT_MSG.used= after the commit. Without Emacs: =git commit -t .git/CLAUDE_COMMIT_MSG=.
- *3, results by call.* Read freely, change only with agreement: the =bs-blee-emacs-server= skill.
  Dblocks that only Blee can expand are regenerated through it.
- *4, VS Code → Emacs.* A user task in =~/.config/Code/User/tasks.json= runs
  =vscode.cs -i bleeOpenFile ${file} ${lineNumber} ${columnNumber}=, bound to a key in
  =keybindings.json=; =bleeOpenFile= takes the socket from the partner file, runs
  =emacsclient -n +LINE:COL FILE= and raises the Emacs frame. =vscodeBleeLinkSnippet= prints the
  task and key for you to paste (VS Code's JSON files take comments, so they are not merged
  automatically). Set =task.saveBeforeRun= to =always=; in Blee, =global-auto-revert-mode=.

* The Layers

[[file:./images/vscodeClaudeLayers.png]]

Each layer has its own small CSXU, so that each gets its own examples menu and its elisp sits on its
own program: =b:vscode.el= over =vscode.cs=, =b:vscode:claude.el= over =vscodeClaude.cs=. The commands
are in the CS-Units =vscode_csu.py= and =vscodeClaude_csu.py=; =vscodeClaude_csu= builds on
=vscode_csu= (its URI, clipboard, paste and =DISPLAY= helpers).

** vscode.cs --- VS Code Itself

| Command                              | Does                                                                                                  |
|--------------------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| =vscodeVersion=                      | version, commit, architecture                                                                         |
| =vscodeTopology=                     | can this shell reach a desktop VS Code? ends with a verdict |
| =vscodeWorkspaces=                   | the folders VS Code has state for, newest first (a history)                                           |
| =vscodeExtensions=                   | installed extensions with versions                                                                    |
| =vscodeExtensionVersion <id>=        | one extension's version (e.g. =anthropic.claude-code=)                                                |
| =vscodeFocus [folder]=               | bring a VS Code window (of that folder) to the front                                                  |
| =vscodeOpenFile <file> [line [col]]= | open at a line and column (=--reuse-window --goto=)                                                   |
| =vscodeOpenFolder <dir>=             | open a folder, =--window=reuse= or =new=                                                              |
| =vscodeAddFolder <dir>=              | add a folder to the current window's workspace                                                        |
| =vscodeDiff <a> <b>=                 | the diff view of two files                                                                            |
| =vscodeOpenUri <uri>=                | hand any URI to VS Code (=code --open-url=)                                                           |
| =bleeOpenFile <file> [line [col]]=   | /(planned, WorkPlan 10.9)/ the file in the partner Emacs, at that line                                |

Installing and pinning VS Code and its extensions is =vscode-sbom.pcs=, not this.

** vscodeClaude.cs --- Its Claude Sessions

| Command                  | Does                                                                         |
|--------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| =claudeSessionsList=     | the running sessions, newest first; =--mode== local, remote or all; =--cwd== |
| =claudeSessionsLatest=   | the id of the most recently active session (the default target)              |
| =claudePromptUri <text>= | the =/open= URI only; sends nothing                                          |
| =claudePrompt <text>=    | the prompt into a session's input box; =--session== an id or new; =--cwd==   |

